														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2509056">
									

<p>To be blunt, its most of the interviews. I'll include some examples below:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Agent de Souza: Pedro de Souza. Agent of Mobile Task Force Alpha-7. [pauses] Wait, you should be telling me your name.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This makes the agent come across as dopey. You'd think with the vast experience with reality benders and other such individuals, that the Foundation has, once the compulsion took place the first time the Agent would have made a line straight to the door so that no other classified info needs to be revealed.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Agent de Souza: I'm supposed to be interrogating you instead, but you decided to make a mockery out of this process.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Reading this transcript its this agent who is making the bigger mockery at this point.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Agent de Souza: You will be helping us by staying here and letting us do research on you without hindering us!</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This isn't so much on the nose as it is a punch to the face. I realize that this is exactly what the Foundations does in a nut shell, but you'd think the Agent would avoid being so blunt, especially when the anomaly has already displayed its interest in cooperating with the Foundation.</p>
<p>It really just goes on and on from there. I realize that there is a compulsion effect in play here, but you'd think the Agent would be more responsible than they are here. If the interview was more of a cat and mouse game in which the Agent was attempting to prevent completely putting all his cards on the table, that would be interesting. As it is, however, this dialogue just comes across as narmy.</p>

							</div>

								
					
